    {
      "source_url": "https://x.com/ohthisis/status/2081694717243519464",
      "text": "It is deeply unsettling to see how quickly the narrative shifts away from the tragedy itself. When safety becomes a political tool, it only deepens the divide and leaves the real issues unaddressed. We need a more honest conversation about accountability and the current state of public security.",
      "author_handle": "ohthisis",
      "created_at": "2026-07-27T10:54:31Z",
      "has_media": false
    },
    {
      "source_url": "https://x.com/TheOriginalBPP/status/2082207283694407694",
      "text": "They need to just stay out of politics and report the news, e.g. illegal immigrant violent attacks, Jews being targeted by Muslim extremists, senior government officials telling the public one thing and the documenting something different, listening to an ex CIC share classified info with ghost writer.",
      "author_handle": "TheOriginalBPP",
      "created_at": "2026-07-28T20:51:16Z",
      "has_media": false
    },
    {
      "source_url": "https://x.com/JaquanTheLibra/status/2082271647059349650",
      "text": "Was a local news writer:\n\n1. Woman called in during a prison riot, said her son was going to be killed. She didn’t know what to do about the situation so I just talked to her for 30 minutes. He was killed.\n\n2. A baby got shot. As soon as I walked in, the news director was eager for me to write the story to beat the competition. I quit that week.",
      "author_handle": "JaquanTheLibra",
      "created_at": "2026-07-29T01:07:02Z",
      "has_media": false
    },
    {
      "source_url": "https://x.com/JasminLaine/status/2014817429625381084",
      "text": "We live in a world where panic reactions are praised. Emotional outrage is rewarded, and\ninstant \"hot\" takes are treated like trustworthy insight.\n\nMeanwhile, critical thinking is dismissed as weakness and nuance is treated like hesitation.\n\nWe aren't suffering from a lack of information. what we struggle with, is a lack of pause and what used to be a natural curiosity to get to the bottom of things. Most people never move past the headline... because headlines aren't built to inform.\n\nThey're built to trigger.",
      "author_handle": "JasminLaine",
      "created_at": "2026-01-23T21:48:03Z",
      "has_media": false
    },
    {
      "source_url": "https://x.com/eastdakota/status/2057252747796218333",
      "text": "Have a problem I genuinely would love input on. My wife and I own a local newspaper. In our town the police arrested a member of the community for having what they thought were naked pictures of a juvenile on his phone. Held him for days. Turns out, it was his own kid and none of the pictures were sexual. Terrible.\n\nNewspaper we own reports on it. Just the facts. Also report on when he’s released and there are no charges. Exactly what local news should do.\n\nHe sues us and the local radio station, which had also reported on the arrest. We’re like: dude, we just reported the facts. Radio station settles (not sure terms). We push on because we did nothing wrong and the nature of a newspaper is reporting what happened.\n\nSo we win. No cause of action against the paper. And then we, under statute, get awarded attorneys fees. They amount to tens of thousands of dollars.\n\nSo what’s the right move? I don’t particularly care about the money. But I do want to discourage other caseless plaintiffs against suing us. Has been a costly distraction. But also seems wrong to bankrupt the guy. Genuinely looking for what to do next…",
      "author_handle": "eastdakota",
      "created_at": "2026-05-21T00:10:51Z",
      "has_media": false
    }
  ]
}
